  "last_message": "I implemented `can_cancel` in `larder/cancellation.py`, following `docs/features/cancellation.md`: a member can cancel a pantry order until 2 hours before its pickup slot starts, and not after. The function returns `now <= pickup_starts - CUTOFF`, where `CUTOFF = timedelta(hours=2)`.\n\nI also added `tests/test_cancellation.py`, which checks four times: well before the cutoff, exactly at it, one second after it, and after pickup has started. The full suite passes (6 tests).\n\n**Decision for you:** the doc doesn't say what happens at exactly the 2-hour mark. I read \"until\u2026 and not after\" as including that moment, so a cancel at exactly 2:00 before pickup is allowed. If you'd rather cut off strictly before 2 hours, change `<=` to `<` and update `test_can_cancel_at_cutoff`. Either way, the doc should probably state the boundary.\n\nThe function doesn't check that both datetimes carry the same timezone setting. If one has a timezone and the other doesn't, Python raises a `TypeError` when comparing them.",
